Rosalind,<br />
<br />
We finished the billing testing at Corrigan Weld and Weld's description hol=
ds up. We traced 60 invoices across eight quarters. Every one of them was p=
aid either by client check or by an instruction the client gave its own cus=
todian. There is no fee debit file, no standing authorization, no template =
with withdrawal language in it. Whatever else this firm has, it does not ha=
ve the fee-debit problem.<br />
<br />
It does have a custody problem, and it comes from the affiliate. Cranleigh =
Trust Company is a related person under common control with the adviser, it=
 is a qualified custodian, and it holds client funds and securities in conn=
ection with advisory services the firm provides. That is custody imputed to=
 the adviser under Rule 206(4)-2(d)(2). The consequences are the surprise e=
xamination under paragraph (a)(4) and, because the related person is the cu=
stodian, the internal control report under paragraph (a)(6)(ii). The firm h=
as neither and has answered Item 9 "no."<br />
<br />
I want the finding drafted so that it cannot be misread. This is not an adv=
iser that took fees out of client accounts without authority. It is an advi=
ser whose sister company is the custodian. Two different exposures under th=
e same rule and completely different remediation.<br />
